nce-anchor-link" id=3D"SonicautoDevelopmentGuide-_Toc56156385"><span class=
=3D"confluence-anchor-link" id=3D"_Toc56156385"></span></span>User Authenti=
cation</h3>
<p local-id=3D"a925bfe00c02">When a user login, Sonicauto will use LDAP to =
authenticate the credentials first and return a JSON Web Token when the aut=
hentication succeed. Once the user is logged in, each subsequent request wi=
ll include the Token.</p>
<h4 local-id=3D"a93eb6f2caf2" id=3D"SonicautoDevelopmentGuide-LDAPAuthentic=
ation">LDAP Authentication</h4>
<p local-id=3D"c1057a54f822"><br></p>
<p>Sonicauto3.0 use python-ldap module to do LDAP Authentication. python-ld=
ap provides an object-oriented API to access LDAP directory servers from Py=
thon programs. The ldap_auth function in Sonicauto is given below and we wi=
ll introduce several main APIs of the module. <br class=3D"atl-forced-newli=
ne"><em>ldap.initialize</em> <br class=3D"atl-forced-newline">
 initializes a new connection object for accessing the given LDAP server, a=
nd return an LDAP object used to perform operations on that server. <br cla=
ss=3D"atl-forced-newline"><em>LDAPObject.simple_bind_s</em> <br class=3D"at=
l-forced-newline">
 After an LDAP object is created, and before any other operations can be at=
tempted over the connection, a bind operation must be performed.<br>
 This method attempts to bind with the LDAP server using either simple auth=
entication, or Kerberos (if available). So the parameters are the username =
and password to be authenticated.<br>
 Once the&lt;ac:structured-macro ac:name=3D"anchor" ac:schema-version=3D"1"=
 ac:macro-id=3D"da0d70ac-c695-4b03-8f4a-da3af661c853"&gt;&lt;ac:parameter a=
c:name=3D""&gt;OLE_LINK20&lt;/ac:parameter&gt;&lt;/ac:structured-macro&gt;&=
lt;ac:structured-macro ac:name=3D"anchor" ac:schema-version=3D"1" ac:macro-=
id=3D"1104f550-86f1-4a97-87bf-d339b2ae01a1"&gt;&lt;ac:parameter ac:name=3D"=
"&gt;OLE_LINK21&lt;/ac:parameter&gt;&lt;/ac:structured-macro&gt; authentica=
tion succeed, it will return a tuple (97, [], 1, []) and raise ldap.INVALID=
_CREDENTIALS {'desc': u'Invalid credentials'} if failed. But the problem he=
re is that when you pass in a null value, the authentication can succeed to=
o. So we add search operation to further confirm the authentication. <br cl=
ass=3D"atl-forced-newline"><em>LDAPObject.search</em> <br class=3D"atl-forc=
ed-newline">
 Perform an LDAP search operation, with base as the DN of the entry at whic=
h to start the search, scope being one of SCOPE_BASE (to search the object =
itself), SCOPE_ONELEVEL (to search the object's immediate children), or SCO=
PE_SUBTREE (to search the object and all its descendants). <br class=3D"atl=
-forced-newline"><em>LDAPObject.result</em> <br class=3D"atl-forced-newline=
">
 This method is used to wait for and return the result of an operation prev=
iously initiated by one of the LDAP asynchronous operations (e.g. search(),=
 modify(), etc.) <br class=3D"atl-forced-newline"><span class=3D"image-wrap=
" style=3D""><img src=3D"f91a112b3480f60e292399711eeceefe701e8afd54a78377bb=
4ea959d2994635" height=3D"320" width=3D"466" style=3D"border: 0px solid bla=
ck"></span> <br class=3D"atl-forced-newline">
 For more details, you can access <a href=3D"https://www.python-ldap.org/en=
/latest/reference/ldap.html">https://www.python-ldap.org/en/latest/referenc=
e/ldap.html</a></p>
<p local-id=3D"0192eb7b23d3"><br></p>
<h4 local-id=3D"693a761cf13a" id=3D"SonicautoDevelopmentGuide-JSONWebTokenA=
uthentication">JSON Web Token Authentication</h4>
<p local-id=3D"a586f0a00edf">JSON Web Token (JWT) is an open standard (RFC =
7519) that defines a compact and self-contained way for securely transmitti=
ng information between parties as a JSON object. This information can be ve=
rified and trusted because it is digitally signed.&nbsp;JWTs can be signed =
using a secret (with the&nbsp;HMAC&nbsp;algorithm) or a public/private key =
pair using&nbsp;RSA&nbsp;or&nbsp;ECDSA. <br>
Authentication is the most common scenario for using JWT. Once the user is =
logged in, each subsequent request will include the JWT, allowing the user =
to access routes, services, and resources that are permitted with that toke=
n. Single Sign On is a feature that widely uses JWT nowadays, because of it=
s small overhead and its ability to be easily used across different domains=
. <br>
In its compact form, JWT consist of three parts separated by dots(.), which=
 are:Header, Payload and Signature. Therefore, a JWT typically looks like "=
xxxxx.yyyyy.zzzzz".<br><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper conf=
luence-embedded-manual-size"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image" heigh=
t=3D"114" width=3D"385" loading=3D"lazy" src=3D"069189e6b0739515316f28519fc=
235262b4891a5ca4784939f6856976bef5e4c" data-image-src=3D"https://sonicwall.=
atlassian.net/wiki/download/attachments/2274492531/worddavf1ab4a1c2b2bdd00f=
b6c97f5d15ec027.png?version=3D1&amp;modificationDate=3D1775532000865&amp;ca=
cheVersion=3D1&amp;api=3Dv2" data-unresolved-comment-count=3D"0" data-linke=
d-resource-id=3D"2274492732" data-linked-resource-version=3D"1" data-linked=
-resource-type=3D"attachment" data-linked-resource-default-alias=3D"worddav=
f1ab4a1c2b2bdd00fb6c97f5d15ec027.png" data-base-url=3D"https://sonicwall.at=
lassian.net/wiki" data-linked-resource-content-type=3D"image/png" data-link=
ed-resource-container-id=3D"2274492531" data-linked-resource-container-vers=
ion=3D"1" data-media-id=3D"7325660b-fed6-4dcb-ab7a-b71b6fd532be" data-media=
-type=3D"file"></span> <br>
In authentication, when the user successfully logs in using their credentia=
ls, a JSON Web Token will be returned. Since tokens are credentials, great =
care must be taken to prevent security issues. In general, you should not k=
eep tokens longer than required. <br>
Whenever the user wants to access a protected route or resource, the user a=
gent should send the JWT, typically in the&nbsp;Authorization&nbsp;header u=
sing the&nbsp;Bearer&nbsp;(this field can be custom named in Django rest fr=
amework) schema.<span class=3D"confluence-anchor-link" id=3D"SonicautoDevel=
opmentGuide-OLE_LINK12"><span class=3D"confluence-anchor-link" id=3D"OLE_LI=
NK12"></span></span><span class=3D"confluence-anchor-link" id=3D"SonicautoD=
evelopmentGuide-OLE_LINK13"><span class=3D"confluence-anchor-link" id=3D"OL=
E_LINK13"></span></span><span class=3D"confluence-anchor-link" id=3D"Sonica=
utoDevelopmentGuide-OLE_LINK14"><span class=3D"confluence-anchor-link" id=
=3D"OLE_LINK14"></span></span><span class=3D"confluence-anchor-link" id=3D"=
SonicautoDevelopmentGuide-OLE_LINK15"><span class=3D"confluence-anchor-link=
" id=3D"OLE_LINK15"></span></span> The content of the header should look li=
ke the following: <br>
Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt; <br>
This can be, in certain cases, a stateless authorization mechanism. The ser=
ver's protected routes will check for a valid JWT in the&nbsp;Authorization=
&nbsp;header, and if it's present, the user will be allowed to access prote=
cted resources. If the JWT contains the necessary data, the need to query t=
he database for certain operations may be reduced, though this may not alwa=
ys be the case. <br>
If the token is sent in the&nbsp;Authorization&nbsp;header, Cross-Origin Re=
source Sharing (CORS) won't be an issue as it doesn't use cookies. <br>
Please access <a class=3D"external-link" href=3D"https://jwt.io/introductio=
n/" rel=3D"nofollow">https://jwt.io/introduction/</a> for more information.=
</p>
